//? Title: Kernel Regression: Local Weights, Bandwidth and Coverage
//? Subtitle: Worked Nadaraya–Watson smoothers, a known-function check and held-out iris predictions
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
A response may vary smoothly with predictors without following one global line or a
chosen parametric curve. We want predictions that borrow information locally while
showing when nearby observations are scarce. Nadaraya–Watson kernel regression forms
nonnegative weighted response averages, with bandwidth setting their locality.
Three-point calculations expose the weights, boundary bias and the distinction between
ordinary and leave-one-out fits. Duplicate predictors prevent interpolation of every
response as bandwidth vanishes. A compact-window example shows why bandwidths must not
be ranked after silently dropping unsupported validation rows. We then check a known
function and analyse iris petal length from petal width, separating full-data smoothing
and conditional bandwidth tuning from an independent 120/30 train/test split. Coverage,
local effective sample size and extrapolation behaviour accompany error summaries.
)

\toc

> The method

>> A local constant minimizes a weighted least-squares problem

For training predictors \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\), responses \(y_i\) and query \(x\),
choose a positive bandwidth \(h\) and nonnegative radial kernel. In the metric used by
this implementation, \(u_i=\|z(x)-z(x_i)\|_2/h\), where \(z\) is the frozen resolved
formula design, optionally standardized. Differentiating the local constant objective gives

$$
 \hat m_h(x)=\arg\min_c\sum_i K(u_i)(y_i-c)^2
 =\frac{\sum_iK(u_i)y_i}{\sum_iK(u_i)}
 =\sum_iw_i(x)y_i,\qquad w_i(x)=\frac{K(u_i)}{\sum_jK(u_j)}.
$$
\alttext(The local constant minimizes a weighted sum of squared residuals. Differentiating sets the weighted residual sum to zero, giving normalized nonnegative weights and a weighted response average.)

If the denominator is positive, weights sum to one. Constants are reproduced exactly,
and predictions lie between the smallest and largest observed response. This prevents
ordinary local-constant regression from extending a linear trend beyond the observed
response range. It does not reproduce every affine function: at a boundary, most available
predictors lie to one side of the query. A Taylor expansion gives the first-order term
\(m'(x)\sum_iw_i(x)(x_i-x)\), which need not vanish. Under regular one-dimensional
sampling, symmetry gives interior bias of order \(h^2\); one-sided boundary weighting can
leave bias of order \(h\). Local linear regression addresses that term, but it is a
different estimator from the one fitted here.

>> Kernel shape, units and scaling

The four kernels use the Euclidean radius \(u\ge0\):

$$
 K_G(u)=e^{-u^2/2},\quad K_E(u)=\tfrac34(1-u^2)\mathbf1_{u<1},\quad
 K_U(u)=\mathbf1_{u\le1},\quad K_T(u)=(1-u)\mathbf1_{u<1}.
$$
\alttext(Gaussian weights decay exponentially in squared radius. Epanechnikov and triangular weights taper to zero at radius one, while uniform weights include that boundary.)

Multiplying a kernel by any positive normalizing constant does not change the average.
Compact support is a ball in the resolved predictor space, not a product box across
coordinates. Gaussian product kernels with a common bandwidth give the same weights as
this radial Gaussian; compact product kernels generally do not.

With standardization enabled, the fit uses training-column means and population SDs:
\(z_{ij}=(X_{ij}-\bar X_j)/\sqrt{n^{-1}\sum_i(X_{ij}-\bar X_j)^2}\).
Exact constant columns use scale one. The bandwidth is then dimensionless. With scaling
disabled it is in raw design units; a single scalar is scientifically meaningful only
with a defensible metric across columns. Formula transformations and dummy coding also
change that metric. No intercept column is needed for local averaging.

Training nulls in required predictors or the response are omitted jointly; retained
values must be finite. `kept_row_indices()` preserves original zero-based row alignment.
Prediction keeps every query row: missing/nonfinite predictors or empty compact windows
give NaN and an explicit status in `prediction_details()`. Unknown categorical levels
raise an error. A Gaussian has mathematical support everywhere, but support is not data
proximity or a guarantee of useful extrapolation. Relative log weights preserve its
normalized local mean even when all original exponentials would underflow.

>> The smoother matrix and conditional complexity

For a fixed design and fixed \(h\), ordinary predictions at training points satisfy
\(\hat y=Sy\), where \(S_{ij}=w_j(x_i)\). Its rows sum to one, but S generally is
neither symmetric nor an orthogonal projection. `smoothing_matrix()` exposes it.
Diagonal entries measure own-response influence and \(\operatorname{tr}(S)\) is the
conditional linear-smoother degrees of freedom. Bandwidth selection depends on responses,
so this trace does not include the complexity cost of searching for h.

For independent homoskedastic errors with variance \(\sigma^2\), fixed query weights give

$$
 \operatorname{Var}(\hat m_h(x)\mid X)=\sigma^2\sum_iw_i(x)^2
 =\frac{\sigma^2}{n_{\rm eff}(x)},\qquad
 n_{\rm eff}(x)=\frac1{\sum_iw_i(x)^2}.
$$
\alttext(Conditional variance is the error variance times squared normalized weights. The reciprocal squared-weight sum is a local effective sample size, between one and the number of observations.)

This is a concentration diagnostic, not a confidence interval: it does not estimate
noise variance or account for smoothing bias, heteroskedasticity or tuning. Small local
neighbourhoods and multiple predictors can make effective sample sizes small; under a
regular p-dimensional design the number of local points is roughly proportional to
\(nh^p\), expressing the dimensionality penalty.

>> Deleting a response is different from fitting it

At fixed preprocessing and bandwidth, removing only observation i yields

$$
 \hat y_i^{(-i)}=\frac{\hat y_i-S_{ii}y_i}{1-S_{ii}},\qquad
 y_i-\hat y_i^{(-i)}=\frac{y_i-\hat y_i}{1-S_{ii}}.
$$
\alttext(The leave-one-out mean removes the observation's own contribution and renormalizes the remaining weights. The residual identity divides the ordinary residual by one minus its leverage.)

This identity requires positive remaining weight. Numerically, \(S_{ii}\) can round to
one at tiny bandwidths even though a Gaussian deletion still has a well-defined relative
weighted mean. The implementation therefore computes deletions directly, excluding the
row before normalizing. `fitted_values()` retains its historical deletion meaning;
`full_fitted_values()` explicitly exposes ordinary full-sample fits.

With distinct predictor locations and positive self-weight, ordinary predictions tend
toward interpolation as \(h\to0\). With duplicates they instead approach each duplicate
location's response mean; deleting one flower retains the other flowers at that width.
As \(h\to\infty\), full fits tend toward the global response mean and deletion fits
toward the corresponding mean excluding each observation. A small apparent error is a
description of the smoother, not a test-set performance estimate.

>> Bandwidth selection must compare the same validation rows

Negative `bandwidth` requests a search. The default grid has 50 log-spaced values from
\(0.01\sqrt p\) to \(2\sqrt p\) in the chosen metric units; an explicit positive
`bandwidth_candidates` vector overrides that grid. The first minimum in caller order wins.
The grid is finite, not a global optimization guarantee, and boundary choices deserve inspection.

For each candidate, `bandwidth_diagnostics()` reports deletion coverage, conditional MSE
on finite predictions and a selection objective. The objective is the mean square error
only when all n deletion predictions are finite; otherwise it is infinity. A wholly
ineligible grid raises an error. This prevents a narrow compact kernel from winning by
refusing its difficult rows. Fixed-bandwidth models may still have partial coverage;
`rmse()` and `r_squared()` then describe the covered subset, and `model_summary()` shows
its size. No coverage or zero response variation gives an undefined R-squared, not one.

The full-training design, transforms and scaling stay fixed during these deletions.
This is conditional response-deletion CV, not a refit of the whole preprocessing pipeline
inside each fold. Its minimum is also optimistically selected on those same responses.
The held-out iris analysis below fits all preprocessing and selects h using training
rows only, then evaluates the untouched test rows. Its training-mean baseline uses no test
responses to make predictions; evaluation R-squared uses test-response variation only
as its stated denominator.

Fixed-bandwidth training costs \(O(n^2p)\), a B-candidate search \(O(Bn^2p)\), and m
new queries \(O(mnp)\). The model stores \(O(np+n+B)\) values. Explicit weight/smoother
matrices add \(O(mn)\) or \(O(n^2)\) output storage; they are diagnostics rather than a
required dense-matrix implementation of every prediction.

> Simple examples

>> Three points reveal the exact weights

Use \(x=(-1,0,1)\), \(y=(0,1,0)\), raw units and Gaussian \(h=1\). Let
\(a=e^{-1/2}\), \(b=e^{-2}\). The ordinary smoother matrix is

$$
 S=\begin{pmatrix}
 1/(1+a+b)&a/(1+a+b)&b/(1+a+b)\\
 a/(1+2a)&1/(1+2a)&a/(1+2a)\\
 b/(1+a+b)&a/(1+a+b)&1/(1+a+b)
 \end{pmatrix}.
$$
\alttext(The middle query gives equal Gaussian weights to its two neighbours. Endpoint queries have one unit self-weight, one e-to-minus-one-half weight and one e-to-minus-two weight, all normalized by their row sum.)

Thus \(\hat y_1=1/(1+2a)\) at the central point, whereas its deleted prediction is
zero because both remaining responses are zero. At an endpoint the ordinary fit is
\(a/(1+a+b)\), while deletion gives \(a/(a+b)\). The full matrix is row-stochastic,
but its differently normalized rows demonstrate why it is not generally symmetric.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kreg_three_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-1,0,1});d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,1,0});
  dm::stats::KernelRegressionOptions o;o.bandwidth=1;o.standardize=false;const dm::stats::KernelRegression m(d,"y ~ x",o);
  const auto s=m.smoothing_matrix();dm::dstruct::DataFrame weights;for(std::size_t j=0;j<3;++j)weights.add_column("weight_"+std::to_string(j),s.col(j));
  auto local=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),fits=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();local.bars({-1,0,1},s.row(1),"weights at x = 0",{37,99,235},.5).title("Normalized central-query weights").x_label("training x").y_label("weight").y_limits(0,.7);
  fits.points({-1,0,1},{0,1,0},"actual",{0,0,0}).points({-1,0,1},m.full_fitted_values(),"ordinary fit",{37,99,235}).points({-1,0,1},m.fitted_values(),"deleted fit",{220,38,38});fits.title("Fitting a response versus deleting it").x_label("x").y_label("response").y_limits(-.1,1.65);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(local).add(fits).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kreg_three_points.svg");
  std::print("Smoother rows:\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",weights.to_md(6),m.training_details().select({"row","actual","full_fit","loo_fit","leverage","effective_n"}).to_md(6));
}
```
