\alttext(Three central-query weights sum to one. A second panel distinguishes observed responses from ordinary full-sample and directly deleted predictions at each location.)

>> Boundary bias and limited extrapolation

Now take \(x=y=(0,1,2)\), Gaussian \(h=1\), without standardization. The true line
passes through every observation, but the ordinary fit at zero is
\((a+2b)/(1+a+b)>0\). At one, symmetric weights reproduce one. Far to the right,
relative Gaussian weights concentrate on the largest predictor and the mean tends to
two; far left it tends to zero. This is a stable prediction rule with poor linear
extrapolation, not evidence that the response is known precisely outside the data.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kreg_boundary.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1,2});d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,1,2});dm::stats::KernelRegressionOptions o;o.bandwidth=1;o.standardize=false;const dm::stats::KernelRegression m(d,"y ~ x",o);
  const auto x=dm::seql(-1,3,241);dm::dstruct::DataFrame q;q.add_column("x",x.values());const auto detail=m.prediction_details(q);
  auto fit=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),concentration=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();fit.lines(x.values(),x.values(),"true line",{0,0,0}).lines(x.values(),m.predict(q),"local constant",{37,99,235}).points({0,1,2},{0,1,2},"training",{220,38,38});fit.title("A local constant bends at boundaries").x_label("query x").y_label("response").y_limits(-1.2,4.5);
  concentration.lines(x.values(),detail.numeric_column("effective_n").values(),"effective n",{37,99,235}).title("Weight concentration changes with x").x_label("query x").y_label("1 / sum(weight squared)").y_limits(0,4);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame extreme;extreme.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{-1000,0,1,2,1000});
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(fit).add(concentration).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kreg_boundary.svg");
  auto table=m.prediction_details(extreme);table.add_column("x",extreme.numeric_column("x").values());std::print("{}\n",table.select({"x","prediction","effective_n","nearest_distance","status"}).to_md(6));
}
