> Iris petal measurements

>> Data and a full-data smoothing illustration

`dm::datasets::iris()` has 150 complete flowers, 50 per species, four measurements in
centimetres and a species label. We use the numeric formula `Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width`.
Species helps describe the sample and stratify the later split; it is not a predictor.
Petal width takes repeated recorded values, so the distinct-location interpolation
argument does not apply to every response separately.

The next fit uses all 150 rows to illustrate the curve, training-only standardization,
bandwidth path and local influence. Its conditional deletion RMSE is a training selection
diagnostic. Because h is chosen using these same deletion losses, the minimum is not
an independent estimate of the selected model's test performance. The bandwidth plot
uses log10 h and only finite full-coverage objectives.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kreg_iris_fit.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();const dm::stats::KernelRegression m(d,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");
  const auto summary=d|dm::group_by("Species")|dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","mean_width_cm"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","mean_length_cm"));
  auto fit=m.plot_fit(d).title("Iris local means from all 150 flowers").y_limits(0,9),cv=m.plot_cv_curve().title("Conditional bandwidth selection");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(fit).add(cv).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kreg_iris_fit.svg");
  std::print("{} flowers, {} columns; {} distinct recorded petal widths.\n\n{}\n\n{}\n",d.nrows(),d.ncols(),dm::unique(d.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values()).size(),summary|dm::to_md(3),m.model_summary().select({"bandwidth","loo_coverage","loo_rmse","loo_r_squared","apparent_rmse","effective_df","search_boundary"}).to_md(6));
}
```
