| virginica | 50 | 2.026 | 5.552 |


| bandwidth | loo_coverage | loo_rmse | loo_r_squared | apparent_rmse | effective_df | search_boundary |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.166324 | 1 | 0.390554 | 0.950725 | 0.370136 | 7.079904 | 0 |

\image(figures/kreg_iris_fit.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The full-data fit and conditional bandwidth path are descriptive training diagnostics. The separate holdout below evaluates the frozen procedure.)
\alttext(Petal lengths and a smoothed curve are plotted against petal width. A second panel marks the selected finite deletion-loss minimum on a log-bandwidth axis; tables give species summaries and conditional complexity.)

The full-data Gaussian choice is h = 0.166324 standardized units, with complete deletion
coverage, conditional RMSE 0.390554 cm and trace(S) about 7.08. Apparent RMSE is
0.370136 cm. These conditional values describe the chosen smoother and are kept separate
from the next test-set calculation.

>> Train first, then evaluate untouched flowers

We prespecify a 120/30 split using `train_test_split(iris,.2,42,"Species")`, leaving ten
test flowers per species. The Gaussian width-only model is the primary procedure.
Three other kernels on that same predictor, and a Gaussian width-plus-sepal-length model,
are declared sensitivity comparisons. Each learns its means/scales and searches its own
bandwidth grid on the training frame only. Test outcomes do not choose h, predictors or
kernel. Comparing their test scores is descriptive on one small shared split, not a
stable ranking of methods across populations.

The baseline predicts the training response mean for every test flower. `score()` reports
coverage, RMSE, MAE and R-squared, with errors conditional on available predictions.
We retain coverage in every comparison because full training-deletion coverage does not
ensure a compact kernel covers every future query. The historical species-stratified
sample is not a random treatment experiment or a general population prevalence sample.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kreg_iris_holdout.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
const auto [train,test]=dm::stats::train_test_split(dm::datasets::iris(),.2,42,"Species");dm::dstruct::DataFrame results;
  const std::vector<dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel> kernels={dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel::Gaussian,dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel::Epanechnikov,dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel::Uniform,dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel::Triangular};
  const std::vector<std::string> labels={"Gaussian width","Epanechnikov width","Uniform width","Triangular width"};
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<kernels.size();++i){dm::stats::KernelRegressionOptions o;o.kernel=kernels[i];const dm::stats::KernelRegression m(train,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width",o);auto row=m.score(test);row.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{labels[i]});row.add_column("bandwidth",std::vector<double>{m.bandwidth()});row.add_column("training_loo_rmse",std::vector<double>{m.rmse()});results=i?results.concat_rows(row):row;}
  const dm::stats::KernelRegression wider(train,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width + Sepal.Length"),primary(train,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");auto extra=wider.score(test);extra.add_column("model",std::vector<std::string>{"Gaussian width + sepal"});extra.add_column("bandwidth",std::vector<double>{wider.bandwidth()});extra.add_column("training_loo_rmse",std::vector<double>{wider.rmse()});results=results.concat_rows(extra);
  const auto actual=test.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),prediction=primary.predict(test);auto comparison=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),residual=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();comparison.points(actual,prediction,"Gaussian width",{37,99,235}).abline(0,1).title("Thirty held-out flowers").x_label("actual petal length (cm)").y_label("prediction (cm)").y_limits(0,9);
  auto details=primary.prediction_details(test);details.add_column("residual",test.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values());details=details.mutate_with("residual",[](const auto& r){return r.get_double("residual")-r.get_double("prediction");});residual.points(test.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values(),details.numeric_column("residual").values(),"test residual",{37,99,235}).abline_h(0).title("Errors against the held-out predictor").x_label("petal width (cm)").y_label("actual - prediction (cm)").y_limits(-2,3);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(comparison).add(residual).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kreg_iris_holdout.svg");
  std::print("{} training, {} test rows.\n\n{}\n",train.nrows(),test.nrows(),results.select({"model","bandwidth","training_loo_rmse","n_predicted","coverage","rmse","mae","r_squared","mean_baseline_rmse"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
120 training, 30 test rows.

| model | bandwidth | training_loo_rmse | n_predicted | coverage | rmse | mae | r_squared | mean_baseline_rmse |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| Gaussian width | 0.149278 | 0.371281 | 30 | 1 | 0.456914 | 0.347127 | 0.932084 | 1.753375 |
| Epanechnikov width | 0.318210 | 0.371309 | 30 | 1 | 0.456002 | 0.347820 | 0.932355 | 1.753375 |
| Uniform width | 0.285597 | 0.373496 | 30 | 1 | 0.452298 | 0.344064 | 0.933449 | 1.753375 |
| Triangular width | 0.395033 | 0.370813 | 30 | 1 | 0.456933 | 0.348079 | 0.932078 | 1.753375 |
| Gaussian width + sepal | 0.292004 | 0.261549 | 30 | 1 | 0.273813 | 0.228794 | 0.975610 | 1.753375 |

\image(figures/kreg_iris_holdout.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Every reported test prediction uses a model trained and tuned on 120 flowers. The same training-mean baseline and coverage columns accompany all five prespecified sensitivity comparisons.)
\alttext(Held-out Gaussian predictions are compared with actual petal lengths and an identity line. A second panel shows test residuals against width, while the table reports all candidate procedures and coverage.)

The primary width-only Gaussian has test RMSE 0.456914 cm, compared with 1.753375 cm
for the training-mean baseline; its selected training deletion RMSE is 0.371281 cm.
All five candidates cover all 30 test flowers. On this split, width-only kernel RMSEs
range from 0.452298 to 0.456933 cm, while adding sepal length gives 0.273813 cm. These
are prespecified sensitivity results on one test set, not a new test-set-driven selection.

>> Local concentration and extrapolation

Finally inspect the full-data Gaussian model over a wider width range. Smooth-looking
curves can hide sparse support. `nearest_distance` is the distance to the closest training
point in the frozen standardized design; `effective_n` measures weight concentration.
Neither supplies an uncertainty interval or validates predictions for biologically
unrepresented flowers. A compact uniform model is included only to expose its explicit
no-support status on the same selected query rows, with its own training-selected h.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/kreg_iris_support.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main(){
const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();const dm::stats::KernelRegression gaussian(d,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width");dm::stats::KernelRegressionOptions o;o.kernel=dm::stats::KernelRegressionKernel::Uniform;const dm::stats::KernelRegression compact(d,"Petal.Length ~ Petal.Width",o);
  const auto x=dm::seql(-1,4,301);dm::dstruct::DataFrame grid;grid.add_column("Petal.Width",x.values());const auto local=gaussian.prediction_details(grid);auto curve=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),support=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();curve.points(d.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values(),d.numeric_column("Petal.Length").values(),"training flowers",{148,163,184}).lines(x.values(),gaussian.predict(grid),"Gaussian mean",{37,99,235}).title("Smoothness does not justify extrapolation").x_label("petal width (cm)").y_label("petal length (cm)").y_limits(0,9);
  support.lines(x.values(),local.numeric_column("effective_n").values(),"effective n",{37,99,235}).title("Local response-weight concentration").x_label("petal width (cm)").y_label("1 / sum(weight squared)").y_limits(0,70);
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame query;query.add_column("Petal.Width",std::vector<double>{-1,.2,.8,1.5,2.5,4,10});auto a=gaussian.prediction_details(query),b=compact.prediction_details(query);a.add_column("width_cm",query.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values());b.add_column("width_cm",query.numeric_column("Petal.Width").values());
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(curve).add(support).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/kreg_iris_support.svg");std::print("Gaussian:\n\n{}\n\nCompact uniform:\n\n{}\n",a.select({"width_cm","prediction","effective_n","nearest_distance","status"}).to_md(6),b.select({"width_cm","prediction","effective_n","nearest_distance","status"}).to_md(6));
}
```
