\image(figures/kreg_iris_support.svg)
// result_end

\caption(A Gaussian mean can remain defined far from the observed predictor range. The tables expose concentration/proximity and distinguish a compact kernel refusing an unsupported query.)
\alttext(A Gaussian curve extends beyond the observed iris widths while staying within the response range. A second panel plots local effective sample size; tables compare Gaussian and compact-window query status.)

At a queried width of 10 cm, the Gaussian prediction is 5.933333 cm and effective_n
is three: its limiting response average comes from the tied widest training flowers.
The nearest standardized design distance is about 9.87, so those three weights do not
represent nearby observations. The uniform kernel explicitly reports no support there.

> Conclusion

Kernel regression is a local-constant weighted average, not a guarantee of interpolation,
linear extrapolation or calibrated uncertainty. The small examples make its row-normalized
weights, leverage, duplicate-location behaviour and deletion coverage inspectable.
Bandwidth selection must use the same validation targets for every candidate. A known
function separates apparent, deletion and reference errors; iris then separates training
selection from a genuinely held-out evaluation. Coverage and local concentration remain
part of the result even when an aggregate RMSE looks attractive.

> References

- Nadaraya, E. A. (1964). [On Estimating Regression|https://doi.org/10.1137/1109020]. ~Theory of Probability and Its Applications~, 9(1), 141–142.
- Watson, G. S. (1964). [Smooth Regression Analysis|https://www.jstor.org/stable/25049340]. ~Sankhyā: The Indian Journal of Statistics, Series A~, 26(4), 359–372.
- Hastie, T. and Loader, C. (1993). [Local Regression: Automatic Kernel Carpentry|https://sites.stat.washington.edu/courses/stat527/s13/readings/hastieloader93.pdf]. ~Statistical Science~, 8(2), 120–143. Local-constant boundary limitations and the local-linear alternative.
- statsmodels developers. [KernelReg|https://www.statsmodels.org/stable/generated/statsmodels.nonparametric.kernel_regression.KernelReg.html]. Documents the `reg_type="lc"` local-constant setting; the default local-linear estimator and its bandwidth selection describe a different procedure.
- R Core Team. [ksmooth|https://stat.ethz.ch/R-manual/R-devel/library/stats/html/ksmooth.html]. The same local-mean family, but a different bandwidth convention based on kernel quartiles; its numerical bandwidth is not interchangeable with h here.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. ~Annals of Eugenics~, 7(2), 179–188. Embedded iris measurements.

